Subject: let the app attach data to an object without script seeing it

What this does
  Adds a way to make a symbol that JavaScript cannot reach. A property stored
  under one is skipped by every way script has of listing an object's keys,
  including Object.getOwnPropertySymbols and JSON.stringify. The engine
  already had such symbols for class private fields; this exposes making one.

Why we need it
  React Native attaches native data to JavaScript objects, and that data must
  be invisible to the app and impossible for it to overwrite. There was no way
  to do that cheaply. The engine's slot for embedder data only exists on
  objects the embedder created itself, and this data has to attach to any
  object, including plain ones that came from script.

  The way around it was to keep a WeakMap and call into it, which means
  entering JavaScript for every read and write. Storing the data as a property
  under a private symbol is a plain property lookup instead. Measured on this
  runtime, in a release build, in nanoseconds per call:

                     WeakMap   private symbol
    read                11.1              4.4
    test for presence   11.5              4.0
    replace             59.5              8.6

  Reading is now cheaper than an ordinary property read, because it looks the
  key up directly instead of going through a string.

What it adds
  JS_NewPrivateSymbol()  - makes a symbol that script can never obtain, and so
                           can never use to read or overwrite the property it
                           keys. Takes a description, which only ever shows up
                           in a debugger.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

Notes
  The symbol is not registered anywhere, so two calls with the same
  description give two different symbols that do not collide.
diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
index d162a8f..7ad7e9b 100644
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -4245,6 +4245,19 @@ static JSValue JS_NewSymbolFromAtom(JSContext *ctx, JSAtom descr,
 }
 
 /* `description` may be pure ASCII or UTF-8 encoded */
+JSValue JS_NewPrivateSymbol(JSContext *ctx, const char *description)
+{
+    JSAtom atom;
+    JSValue symbol;
+
+    atom = JS_NewAtom(ctx, description ? description : "");
+    if (atom == JS_ATOM_NULL)
+        return JS_EXCEPTION;
+    symbol = JS_NewSymbolFromAtom(ctx, atom, JS_ATOM_TYPE_PRIVATE);
+    JS_FreeAtom(ctx, atom);
+    return symbol;
+}
+
